// Word data the T9 game draws from: English words, length 4-8.
//
// Built in layers:
// 1. Start from a broad dictionary word list (words_alpha), filtered to
//    words that are real per WordNet's lemma list (excluding WordNet's
//    'instance' senses — specific named people/places like "Comte" or
//    "Aaron" — so those are excluded while dual-use words like "hammer" or
//    "Iranian" are kept) or a regular inflection of one (stripping a
//    regular -s/-es/-ed/-ing/-er/-est suffix, handling e-drop and doubled
//    consonants) of one, plus a small hand-added list of function words and
//    irregular verb/plural forms WordNet does not index at all (else,
//    could, went, were, women, isle, fled, ...).
// 2. Keep only words where the word ITSELF, or the base it stems from, is
//    common enough to appear in a 50k-word frequency list — e.g. "lamer"
//    passes because its base "lame" is common, even though "lamer" itself
//    narrowly misses the top 50k. This is deliberately looser than gating
//    on the inflected surface form directly (which was rejecting legitimate
//    words like "lamer"), while still excluding most obscure/technical
//    vocabulary that a casual player would find unfair (archaic terms,
//    scientific taxonomy, etc.) since neither the obscure word nor its base
//    tends to be frequent.
// 3. A manual pass over every word this loosening added to WORDS_GOOD (731
//    words), reading each one's actual WordNet definition, removing ones
//    that looked wrong — double inflections ("aboded"), misspellings
//    ("chaped" for "chapped"), nonstandard comparatives ("wronger"),
//    overly obscure/archaic/dialectal terms ("bedlamer", "gentles"), and
//    offensive terms ("dagos") — plus a lighter pattern-based safety scan
//    (not an exhaustive manual review) over the much larger set of newly-
//    added real-but-not-good words, catching things like "fakeer" and a
//    couple of slurs. See exclude_good.txt / exclude_dict_only.txt in the
//    scratchpad this was built from for the exact lists.
//
// DICTIONARY: the full list, used to check whether a typed guess is a real
// word at all (hangman mode).
// WORDS_GOOD: the subset that is also 'good' under the current t9-logic.js
// rules (including wraparound merges, e.g. HINDU), precomputed offline —
// classifying the whole dictionary against every possible circular
// digit-merge takes several seconds, too slow to redo on every page load —
// rather than at runtime. Whenever the good/bad rules in t9-logic.js
// change, or the dictionary itself is regenerated, this needs regenerating
// to match — see the isGoodWord-based filter used to build it.
// WORDS_GOOD_RANK[i] is WORDS_GOOD[i]'s frequency rank (1 = most common) —
// its own rank if it has one, else its base stem's rank. Used to gauge a
// hangman puzzle's difficulty from how rare its rarest valid answer is.
